The CUDA execution model does allow for multiple CUDA applications to share the same GPU. When you launch multiple CUDA programs on a system with only one GPU card, the GPU's driver will interleave the execution of multiple kernels, effectively multitasking between the different applications. This is done in a fair scheduling manner, so no single application should hog the entire GPU to the detriment of others.

However, the execution correctness is not guaranteed if the applications are not properly designed. If two applications try to access or modify the same memory space concurrently, it can cause race conditions and lead to incorrect results or even crashes. Hence, it’s essential to ensure that your CUDA programs are designed to handle such situations safely.

In terms of scheduling tasks, the GPU driver maintains a queue of tasks (kernels to be executed, memory transfers, etc.) for each CUDA context. When a context's task is at the front of the queue, the hardware will execute it. If a context has more than one task in the queue, the GPU will execute them in the order they were queued.

