A good strategy to pass the culture information between DAL DLLs (Data Access Layer) and UI (User Interface) projects is to utilize the localization features provided by .NET. The .NET framework has built-in support for resource management and localization, which can handle the culture information automatically.

In your Core library, you can store the resource files for different cultures. These resource files contain the localized strings, images, or any other type of resource that your application uses. You can organize these resource files using the naming convention such as "ResourceName.CultureName.resx", where "CultureName" represents the specific culture (e.g., en-US for US English).

To access the resource file based on the current culture, you can use the `ResourceManager` class provided by .NET. This class can automatically select the appropriate resource file based on the culture information.

Here's an example of how you can retrieve a localized string from the resource file in your Core library within a UI project:

```csharp
using System.Resources;
using System.Globalization;

public class MyUIForm
{
    private static ResourceManager resourceManager;

    public MyUIForm()
    {
        // Initialize the resource manager with the resource file in the Core library
        resourceManager = new ResourceManager("Core.Resources.MyResources", typeof(MyUIForm).Assembly);
    }

    public string GetLocalizedText(string key)
    {
        // Get the current culture from the CurrentThread or HttpContext
        CultureInfo currentCulture = CultureInfo.CurrentCulture;

        // Retrieve the localized string from the resource file based on the current culture
        string localizedString = resourceManager.GetString(key, currentCulture);

        return localizedString;
    }
}
```

In the above example, the `MyUIForm` class initializes the `ResourceManager` with the resource file name "Core.Resources.MyResources" from the Core library. The `GetLocalizedText` method retrieves the localized string based on the provided key and the current culture.

By utilizing the localization features in .NET, you don't need to manually pass the culture information between DLLs and UI projects. The resource manager will handle the selection of the appropriate resource file based on the current culture, making your application localization-ready and reducing the complexity of managing culture information manually.

