I am working with Django templates and I've encountered an issue with the forloop.first and forloop.last conditions in my template code. Here's my code:

```python
{% for f in friendslist %}

        {% if forloop.first %}
            // display something                            
        {% endif %}

        // display stuff

        {% if forloop.last %}
            // display something
        {% endif %}

{% endfor %}
```

This code works as expected when there is more than one item in the friendslist. However, if there is only one item, the content inside the forloop.last conditional does not display. I'm assuming this is because the loop, in that case, is the first, but it's also the last, right? So why doesn't the content inside both the first and last conditional show?

response:
The behavior you're seeing is actually expected. In Django templating, `forloop.first` and `forloop.last` are used to check if the current loop iteration is the first or the last respectively.

The `forloop.last` is only true if the current loop iteration is the last one. In your case, when there is only one item in the friendslist, the single loop iteration is both the first and the last. However, the `forloop.last` condition checks after the iteration is processed. Thus, it doesn't execute the code in the `forloop.last` block.

Here's a workaround to handle a single item in the friendslist:

```python
{% for f in friendslist %}
    {% if forloop.first %}
        // display something                            
    {% endif %}

    // display stuff

    {% if forloop.last %}
        // display something
    {% endif %}

    {% if forloop.first and forloop.last %}
        // display something for the case when it's both first and last
    {% endif %}
{% endfor %}
```

In this code, when there is only one item in the friendslist, the condition `forloop.first and forloop.last` will be true and the corresponding block of code will be executed.

