// Dynamic Programming implementation of Fibonacci 
// number problem 
int fibonacci(int n) 
{ 
	/* Declare an array to store Fibonacci numbers. */
	int f[n+2];   
	int i; 

	/* 0th and 1st number of the series are 0 and 1*/
	f[0] = 0; 
	f[1] = 1; 

	for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) 
	{ 
		/* Add the previous 2 numbers in the series 
		   and store it */
		f[i] = f[i-1] + f[i-2]; 
	} 

	return f[n]; 
}
```

In this code, we declare an array `f` to store Fibonacci numbers. We initialize the first two numbers of the series (`f[0] = 0` and `f[1] = 1`). Then, we use a loop to calculate the Fibonacci numbers iteratively by adding the previous two numbers in the series and storing the result in `f[i]`. Finally, we return the `n`th Fibonacci number from the array.

This dynamic programming approach reduces the time complexity of computing Fibonacci numbers from exponential to linear, making it significantly more efficient.

