```javascript
function quickSort(arr) {
    if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;
    const pivot = arr[arr.length - 1];
    const left = [];
    const right = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
        if (arr[i] < pivot) {
            left.push(arr[i]);
        } else {
            right.push(arr[i]);
        }
    }

    return [...quickSort(left), pivot, ...quickSort(right)];
}
```

This code defines a recursive function named `quickSort` that takes an array `arr` as input. The function first checks if the length of the array is less than or equal to 1. If so, it simply returns the array as it is already sorted.

If the array has more than 1 element, the function selects the last element of the array as the pivot. It then creates two empty arrays, `left` and `right`, to store the elements that are less than the pivot and greater than or equal to the pivot, respectively.

Next, the function iterates through the elements of the input array excluding the last element (pivot). If an element is less than the pivot, it is added to the `left` array. Otherwise, it is added to the `right` array.

Finally, the function recursively calls itself with the `left` and `right` arrays, and combines the sorted `left`, pivot, and sorted `right` arrays using the spread operator (`...`). This results in a sorted array that is returned as the output.

By calling this `quickSort` function with your list of numbers as the input, you can perform the quicksort operation and obtain the sorted list.

