Introduction
This guide will walk you through creating a Rust program that will use the Rusoto library to build applications with Amazon Web Services. We will explore the capabilities of Rusoto by creating functionality to create and delete files in an S3 bucket, launch EC2 instances, retrieve information from DynamoDB tables, and send messages to SQS queues. We will also securely store sensitive information such as AWS access keys and secrets using environment variables. We will provide clear instructions on how to set up and run the project on a local machine or a server.

Prerequisites
To follow this tutorial, you will need:

- Rust programming language and Cargo package manager
- Amazon Web Services (AWS) account
- AWS CLI and credentials set up
- Environment variables set up for AWS access keys and secrets

Step 1: Create a new Rust project using Cargo
First, we will create a new Rust project using Cargo. Open a terminal window and navigate to the directory where you want to create the project:

$ cd /path/to/directory

Create a new Rust project with Cargo:

$ cargo new rusoto-project

Navigate into the new project directory:

$ cd rusoto-project

Step 2: Add dependencies to Cargo.toml
In Rust, we use the Cargo.toml file to specify our project's dependencies. Open the file with your preferred text editor:

$ code Cargo.toml

Add the following dependencies under `[dependencies]`:

[dependencies]
rusoto_core = "0.47.0"
rusoto_s3 = "0.47.0"
rusoto_ec2 = "0.47.0"
rusoto_dynamodb = "0.47.0"
rusoto_sqs = "0.47.0"
dotenv = "0.15.0"

These dependencies will allow us to use Rusoto to interact with S3, EC2, DynamoDB, and SQS, as well as use dotenv to securely store our AWS access keys and secrets in environment variables. Make sure to save the changes to the Cargo.toml file.

Step 3: Set up environment variables
Storing sensitive information like AWS access keys and secrets directly in your code can lead to security vulnerabilities. Instead, we use environment variables to store this information securely. 

Create a new file called `.env` in the root directory of your project:

$ touch .env

Add the following lines to the `.env` file, replacing `YOUR_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `YOUR_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` with your own AWS access keys:

AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=YOUR_ACCESS_KEY_ID
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=YOUR_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY

Note: If you do not know how to obtain your AWS access keys, visit this link. https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/aws-sec-cred-types.html#access-keys-and-secret-access-keys 

Step 4: Implement functionality using Rusoto
We will implement the following functionality for our Rusoto project:
- Create and delete files in an S3 bucket
- Launch an EC2 instance
- Retrieve information from a DynamoDB table
- Send a message to an SQS queue

Let's create a new file called `main.rs` in the `src` directory:

$ cd src
$ touch main.rs

Open the `main.rs` file in your text editor:

$ code main.rs

Add the following code to import the necessary Rusoto libraries and our dotenv configuration:

